     "explanation": "Im is in + dem — the dative — and the dative after a two-way preposition means location, not movement: they are already inside (schon = already). On the way there would be \"Ich gehe ins Restaurant\" with the accusative ins. Leaving would use aus dem Restaurant.",
     "visual": {
      "type": "diagram",
      "svg": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 1100 1100\" class=\"lesson-visual\"><rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"1100\" height=\"1100\" fill=\"#1a1a2e\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"55\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#ffd700\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"36\">READ THE CASE, NOT THE WORDS</text><rect x=\"200\" y=\"140\" width=\"700\" height=\"140\" rx=\"18\" fill=\"rgba(59,130,246,0.15)\" stroke=\"#3b82f6\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"200\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"30\">Ich bin schon im Restaurant.</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"248\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"20\">a text message from your friend</text><line x1=\"550\" y1=\"290\" x2=\"550\" y2=\"360\" stroke=\"#ffd700\" stroke-width=\"4\"/><polygon points=\"550,380 535,350 565,350\" fill=\"#ffd700\"/><rect x=\"250\" y=\"400\" width=\"600\" height=\"170\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"rgba(255,215,0,0.1)\" stroke=\"#ffd700\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"460\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#ffd700\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"28\">im = in + ???</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"510\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">Which case is hiding inside the contraction,</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"545\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">and what does that case signal?</text><rect x=\"90\" y=\"650\" width=\"440\" height=\"200\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"rgba(245,158,11,0.1)\" stroke=\"#f59e0b\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><text x=\"310\" y=\"710\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#f59e0b\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"24\">ins = in + das</text><text x=\"310\" y=\"755\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">AKKUSATIV - movement</text><text x=\"310\" y=\"800\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"20\">heading there</text><rect x=\"570\" y=\"650\" width=\"440\" height=\"200\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"rgba(16,185,129,0.1)\" stroke=\"#10b981\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><text x=\"790\" y=\"710\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#10b981\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"24\">im = in + dem</text><text x=\"790\" y=\"755\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">DATIV - location</text><text x=\"790\" y=\"800\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"20\">already there</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"940\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"20\">One extra clue in the sentence: schon means already.</text></svg>",
      "caption": "The contraction carries the meaning: ins (accusative) = heading there, im (dative) = already there. Which one did your friend use?"
     }
